1
00:02:12,352 --> 00:02:18,352
Look out pardners, there's a new mouse in town!

2
00:04:24,705 --> 00:04:30,705
An American Tail: Fievel Goes West is a animation,
family, western film directed by Simon Wells.

3
00:06:37,058 --> 00:06:43,058
Some time after the Mousekewitz's have settled in America, they find that
they are still having problems with the threat of cats.

4
00:08:49,411 --> 00:08:55,411
That makes them eager to try another home out in the west, where
they are promised that mice and cats live in peace.

5
00:11:01,764 --> 00:11:07,764
Unfortunately, the one making this claim is an oily con artist named Cat R.

6
00:13:14,117 --> 00:13:20,117
Waul who is intent on his own sinister plan.

7
00:15:26,470 --> 00:15:32,470
Fievel Mousekewitz (voice), played by Phillip Glasser.

8
00:17:38,823 --> 00:17:44,823
Wylie Burp (voice), played by James Stewart.

9
00:19:51,176 --> 00:19:57,176
Mama Mousekewitz (voice), played by Erica Yohn.

10
00:22:03,529 --> 00:22:09,529
Tanya Mousekewitz (voice), played by Cathy Cavadini.

11
00:24:15,882 --> 00:24:21,882
Papa Mousekewitz (voice), played by Nehemiah Persoff.

12
00:26:28,235 --> 00:26:34,235
Tiger (voice), played by Dom DeLuise.

13
00:28:40,588 --> 00:28:46,588
An American Tail: Fievel Goes West (also known as An American Tail 2: Fievel Goes West or An American Tail II) is a 1991 American animated Western musical comedy adventure film directed by Phil Nibbelink and
Simon Wells (in their feature directorial debuts), with producer Steven Spielberg for Amblin Entertainment and animated by his Amblimation animation studio and released by Universal Pictures.

14
00:30:52,941 --> 00:30:58,941
A sequel to 1986's An American Tail, the film follows the story of the
Mousekewitzes, a family of Russian-Jewish mice who emigrate to the Wild West.

15
00:33:05,294 --> 00:33:11,294
In it, Fievel is separated from his family as the train approaches the American Old West; the
film chronicles him and Sheriff Wylie Burp teaching Tiger how to act like a dog.

16
00:35:17,647 --> 00:35:23,647
Fievel Goes West was the first production for the short-lived Amblimation, a studio
Spielberg set up to keep the animators of Who Framed Roger Rabbit (1988) working.

17
00:37:30,000 --> 00:37:36,000
It is also the only Amblimation film to use cel animation, the last in the
series to do so, and the last to be released in theaters.

18
00:39:42,352 --> 00:39:48,352
While the animation medium was transitioning to computers in the late 1980s and early 1990s, Spielberg wanted almost all of
the animation of Fievel Goes West to be hand-drawn, describing animation as "an arts-and-crafts business".

19
00:41:54,705 --> 00:42:00,705
He also wanted the animation to have a "live-action" feel.

20
00:44:07,058 --> 00:44:13,058
Phillip Glasser, Dom DeLuise, Nehemiah Persoff, and Erica Yohn reprise
their roles from the first film for Fievel Goes West.

21
00:46:19,411 --> 00:46:25,411
Tanya's original voice actor, Amy Green, was replaced by Cathy Cavadini, and new characters were
voiced by John Cleese, Amy Irving, Jon Lovitz, and James Stewart in his final film role.

22
00:48:31,764 --> 00:48:37,764
James Horner returned as a composer and wrote the film's song "Dreams
to Dream", which garnered a Golden Globe nomination.

23
00:50:44,117 --> 00:50:50,117
Premiering at the Kennedy Center on November 17, 1991, An American Tail:
Fievel Goes West began its American theatrical run on November 22.

24
00:52:56,470 --> 00:53:02,470
This was the same day Walt Disney Pictures' Beauty and the Beast (1991) was distributed, making
it the third instance of two animated films being released on the same date.

25
00:55:08,823 --> 00:55:14,823
Fievel Goes West was promoted with a wide array of tie-ins
and started in the top ten at the box office.

26
00:57:21,176 --> 00:57:27,176
The film grossed $22 million in the United States
against a budget of $16 million.

27
00:59:33,529 --> 00:59:39,529
Some film journalists and executives attributed this to
having to compete with the Disney film.

28
01:01:45,882 --> 01:01:51,882
Upon its release, Fievel Goes West received mixed reviews from critics: while its animation, score, and voice
performances were praised, most criticisms targeted its story, pacing, and lack of character development.

29
01:03:58,235 --> 01:04:04,235
However, it found success when it came to home video sales, quickly reaching the top of the video charts when released on tape in March 1992; at
the time, the film held the record for shortest theater-to-home-video transfer, and it has since gained a large cult following.

30
01:06:10,588 --> 01:06:16,588
In addition to garnering more home media releases, television airings, and video game adaptations later on, the film has made
numerous 2010s retrospective best-of lists from online publications, especially best Netflix-available Western films.

31
01:08:22,941 --> 01:08:28,941
Fievel Goes West was followed by a short-lived CBS series named Fievel's American Tails and two direct-to-video films: An
American Tail: The Treasure of Manhattan Island (1998) and An American Tail: The Mystery of the Night Monster (1999).

32
01:10:35,294 --> 01:10:41,294
The film explores themes of sheriff, indian territory, mouse, villain, sequel.

33
01:12:47,647 --> 01:12:53,647
The tone is lighthearted.
